{"causes":["Chuzon was born into a prominent family with connections to the imperial court.","He rose to prominence under Emperor Valentinian II, who valued his administrative skills.","The economic crisis of the late 4th century CE created an opportunity for Chuzon's reforms to take effect."],"effects":["His tax reform efforts helped stabilize the eastern provinces and promote trade.","The construction of churches in Antioch solidified the city's status as a major center of Christianity.","The compilation of the Codex Theodosianus provided a comprehensive framework for Roman law that would shape governance for centuries to come."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"valentinian-ii","sourceName":"Valentinian II, Emperor of Rome","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"antiochus-chuzon","targetName":"Antiochus Chuzon, praetorian prefect of the East and consul","context":"Chuzon rose to prominence under Valentinian II's patronage."},{"sourceSlug":"theodosius-i","sourceName":"Theodosius I, Roman Emperor","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"codex-theodosianus","targetName":"Codex Theodosianus, compilation of Roman law","context":"Chuzon contributed to the compilation of the Codex Theodosianus under Theodosius I's patronage."},{"sourceSlug":"antioch","sourceName":"Antioch, city in the Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"churches-in-antioch","targetName":"Churches in Antioch, built during Chuzon's lifetime","context":"Chuzon sponsored the construction of several churches in Antioch."},{"sourceSlug":"constantine-i","sourceName":"Constantine I, Roman Emperor","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"early-christian-church","targetName":"Early Christian Church, with Chuzon as a key figure","context":"Chuzon's support for Christianity helped shape the early Christian Church."},{"sourceSlug":"eastern-roman-empire","sourceName":"Eastern Roman Empire, under Valentinian II and Theodosius I","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"antiochus-chuzon-administration","targetName":"Administration of the Eastern Roman Empire under Chuzon's leadership","context":"Chuzon oversaw the administration of the eastern provinces."},{"sourceSlug":"euphrates-river","sourceName":"Euphrates River, major waterway in the Middle East","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"eastern-provinces","targetName":"Eastern Provinces, governed by Chuzon as praetorian prefect","context":"Chuzon administered territories along the Euphrates River."},{"sourceSlug":"codex-theodosianus","sourceName":"Codex Theodosianus, compilation of Roman law","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"roman-law","targetName":"Roman Law, with Chuzon's contributions as a key factor","context":"The Codex Theodosianus provided a comprehensive framework for Roman law."},{"sourceSlug":"antioch-churches","sourceName":"Churches in Antioch, built during Chuzon's lifetime","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"christianity-in-antioch","targetName":"Christianity in Antioch, with Chuzon as a key figure","context":"Chuzon's support for Christianity helped solidify the city's status as a major center of Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"valentinian-ii-successors","sourceName":"Valentinian II Successors, who ruled the Eastern Roman Empire after his death","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"antiochus-chuzon-successors","targetName":"Antiochus Chuzon Successors, who continued his administrative reforms","context":"Chuzon's reforms and legacy were carried forward by his successors."}],"places":[{"name":"Constantinople (modern-day Istanbul)","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Antioch (modern-day Antakya, Turkey)","role":"Residence"},{"name":"Ephesus (modern-day Selçuk, Turkey)","role":"Administrative Center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Antiochus Chuzon","type":"Reference article","slug":"antiochus-chuzon"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3618741","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiochus_Chuzon"],"tags":["Jurists & Legal Scholars","legal-interpretation","doctrine-development","temporal-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Antiochus Chuzon (fl. 5th century CE) was a Samaritan leader who led a major revolt against Byzantine imperial rule in Palestine around 484–486 CE — one of the most significant Samaritan uprisings in the late antique period and an episode that illustrates both the resilience of Samaritan religious identity under Christian Roman rule and the explosive intersection of taxation, religious coercion, and ethnic autonomy in the late imperial periphery. The Samaritans — the people of ancient Samaria who maintained a distinctive form of Israelite religion centred on Mount Gerizim rather than Jerusalem — had been the subject of increasing Christian imperial pressure since the reign of Constantine, as successive emperors converted Samaritan sacred sites to churches and restricted Samaritan religious practice.

The revolt under Antiochus Chuzon erupted when Byzantine imperial policy — probably involving the forced consecration of the Samaritan synagogue on Mount Gerizim as a church — passed the threshold of tolerance. Antiochus led his followers in armed resistance, reportedly appointing himself king or messianic leader of the Samaritans in an explicitly nationalist and religious framework. Byzantine forces under the command of the dux (military commander) of Palestine eventually suppressed the revolt with considerable violence, imposing additional restrictions on Samaritan religious practice and further reducing their legal standing in the empire. The rebellion was one of several Samaritan revolts in the 4th–7th centuries (the largest occurred in 529–530 and 556 CE) that together represent a sustained pattern of resistance to Christian imperial homogenisation.

Antiochus Chuzon is a figure primarily known from brief references in Byzantine chronicles and anti-Samaritan polemical literature. His revolt contributed to the progressive deterioration of the Samaritan position in the Byzantine Empire — a pressure that ultimately made many Samaritans receptive to the Arab-Muslim conquest of Palestine in 636 CE, which offered greater religious tolerance than Christian Byzantine administration had provided.","subjects":["Late Antiquity","Byzantine Empire","Palestine","Religious History","Resistance","Classical Era","Near East","Minority History"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Antiochus Chuzon's administrative reforms, tax policies, and support for Christianity helped shape the Eastern Roman Empire and early Christian Church.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
